  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 215 AM CDT Tue Sep 1 2026\n\nThe first day of meteorological fall will be a warm one with highs\nin the 80s to lower 90s. The heat index will climb into the lower \nto middle 90s across central into northeast WI. Attention then\nturns to late tonight when a warm front will lift northward\ntowards the area. A 40 knot 850mb low level jet/850mb warm\nadvection will trigger thunderstorm activity across Minnesota and\nwestern WI during the evening, then overspread the area after \nmidnight. Mid-level lapse rates not all that impressive at 5.5 \nto 6 C/KM. However, low level winds at 850mb are expected to\nincrease to about 40 knots towards 12z at Green Bay. Wind shear\nvalues of at least 30-40 knots and CAPE values of 500 to 1,500\nJ/KG would support the potential for strong/damaging wind gusts\nand potential for hail up to one inch in diameter. After the \nstorms exit Wednesday morning, a lot of uncertainty if storms\nwould redevelop across east-central WI during the afternoon.\nOnly have a 20-30% chance into the evening. \n\nFor Thursday into the weekend, above normal temperatures are going\nto continue with the periodic chances of thunderstorms. Hopefully\nsome of these storms will move across our area to help alleviate\nthe on-going drought. Placement of the axis of heavier rain will\nbe difficult to pinpoint this far out based on the upper air \npattern. Otherwise, late guidance is suggesting cooler\ntemperatures early next week, a hint of the changing of the \nseasons.",
